### Map Federal Grant Integrity for Local Procurement

When federal grant funds—such as Department of Homeland Security (DHS) funding—are disbursed to state or local government entities (e.g., counties, municipalities, or emergency management boards), local procurement practices become bound by federal regulatory oversight. Under **2 CFR § 200.318(c)(1)** (Uniform Guidance), non-federal entities administering federal awards must maintain written standards of conduct covering real, potential, or apparent conflicts of interest.

These standards dictate that no employee, officer, or agent may participate in the selection, award, or administration of a contract supported by federal funds if a real or apparent conflict of interest exists—including personal financial ties, prospective employment negotiations, or immediate family involvement. Federal grant conditions operate concurrently with state and local ethics statutes; where standards diverge, the most restrictive compliance standard governs.

